  [1mSection 3 — UI, error handling, testing[0m

  [1mUI.[0m The header gains a bell button with an unread badge; clicking it toggles a panel listing active notifications,
  each with a relative time ("due in 45 minutes" / "overdue by 2 hours") and a dismiss button, plus a "dismiss all".
  Empty state reads "Nothing due." Each task row is: done checkbox, title, a [38;5;153mdatetime-local[39m due field, a watch toggle,
  delete. Overdue entries carry a text label, not just a color, so the distinction survives colorblindness and
  greyscale. The badge lives in an [38;5;153maria-live="polite"[39m region and the bell is labelled "Notifications, N unread" — a
  count that only exists visually is invisible to a screen reader, which defeats the point of a notification.

  [1mError handling.[0m Three failure modes worth designing for:
  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throwing (private mode, quota) — [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m catches, the app runs in memory, and a single inline notice
    says persistence is off. It does not crash.
  - Corrupt stored JSON — parse failure falls back to an empty list [3mand[0m copies the bad blob to a backup key. Silently
    destroying a user's task list because one byte went wrong is the worst outcome available here.
  - A bad or cleared due-date input — coerced to [38;5;153mnull[39m at the [38;5;153mtasks.js[39m boundary, so [38;5;153mNaN[39m never reaches the engine.

  Epoch-ms timestamps make DST a non-issue. A user moving their system clock backward just sees fewer notifications; I'm
  accepting that rather than defending against it.

  [1mTesting[0m, written test-first. The weight sits on [38;5;153mcomputeNotifications[39m, which is pure and takes [38;5;153mnow[39m as an argument, so
  every case is exact and instant: boundary conditions at exactly [38;5;153mdueAt[39m and exactly the window edge, overdue superseding
  due-soon, ack suppression, ack [3minvalidation[0m when a task is rescheduled, exclusion of done/unwatched/undated tasks,
  sort order, empty inputs. Then [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m against a throwing stub and corrupt payloads, [38;5;153mtasks.js[39m CRUD and date
  coercion, and a thin jsdom layer over the render functions for badge count and dismiss wiring.

  Does this look right? If so, I'll write the spec to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-task-notifications-design.md[39m
  (uncommitted) for you to review before we go anywhere near an implementation plan.

